OK.

00:00:57.650 --> 00:00:59.750
Almost everyone in the room.

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:00.500
Thank you.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:02.550
And follow up question--

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:08.100
How many of you are using
some cloud identity provider?

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:08.960
OK pretty good.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:14.520
Pretty good number of
people who are doing that.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.610
So this really
value have probably

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.740
a well-established
identity process

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:21.930
when it comes to your
on premise world.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:23.790
And you're living
in this dual world

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:27.226
of on premise, and then cloud.

00:01:27.226 --> 00:01:29.100
You probably have a
well-established identity

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:33.252
process that works with
the on-prem identity.

00:01:33.252 --> 00:01:35.460
And then you are looking to
extend that to the cloud,

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:37.800
hopefully using this
identity as a service.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:39.831
Cloud-based identity solution.

00:01:39.831 --> 00:01:41.580
And this is what we're
here to talk about.

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:43.200
The agenda here is
to just walk you

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.920
through how Google thinks
about identity as a service.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:49.560
We'll walk you through
our solution in this space

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.076
and why we think
we are different.

00:01:51.076 --> 00:01:52.200
Why should you care, right?

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.930
There are other choices,
obviously, in the market.

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:56.490
We'll walk you through a demo.

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:58.980
And we'll talk a little
bit about Go-to-Market

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:00.150
with partners.

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:04.260
And finally we'll wrap it up
with our closing takeaways.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:07.830
So let's jump right in.

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:09.910
With identity as a service,
we think about this

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:12.770
as across these six categories.

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:15.460
The first being
directory, which is where

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:17.200
you have all your entities.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:19.730
We think about account security.

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:22.730
We think about your devices,
particularly mobile.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:24.140
We think about
your applications.

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:27.440
So the idea that your
end users can sign in

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:29.720
without having to
authenticate again and again.

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:31.657
As well as provisioning.

00:02:31.657 --> 00:02:33.240
And I'll talk a
little bit about that.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:36.050
And finally, some sort of
reporting, analytics, insights,

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:39.080
into what's going on
in this environment.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:42.620
Now this is a
three-day conference.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:45.770
You're probably going to be
seeing hundreds of slides.

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:47.510
So I think there
is zero probability

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:49.610
you're going to remember
these six categories

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:50.930
at the end of Friday.

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:53.750
And so I thought, let's
try a different approach.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:56.440
Let's try to do something that--

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:58.070
let's walk through a life cycle.

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:02.480
And let's imagine that you
have 10 new employees that

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:04.220
are going to be starting.

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:08.060
They need access to a few
cloud-based applications.

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:11.240
They probably have
a mobile device.

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:13.610
So this is an audience
participation exercise, so

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:14.840
work with me on this.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:16.850
What is it that you
think the first step you

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:20.450
got to do if you are an admin,
and you have 10 new employees

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:22.070
that are being onboarded?

00:03:22.070 --> 00:03:23.051
Yes, please.

00:03:23.051 --> 00:03:24.175
AUDIENCE: In the HR system.

00:03:24.175 --> 00:03:25.340
KARTHIK LAKSHMINARAYANAN:
In the HR system.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:26.240
Fantastic answer.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:26.820
Thank you.

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:28.010
That is the right answer.

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:31.280
Of course, every answer the
audience gives is correct.

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:35.420
But that is absolutely the first
step that you would have to do.

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:37.250
There's an HR system,
maybe something

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:39.892
like Work Day or something
else that people are using.

00:03:39.892 --> 00:03:41.600
And so these accounts
need to be created.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:43.475
So that's where the
directory pieces come in.

00:03:43.475 --> 00:03:46.760
Because you've got to sync this
into some [INAUDIBLE] store,

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:49.357
which is where the
identity pieces go in.

00:03:49.357 --> 00:03:50.690
This is where the user comes in.

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:52.790
Then the next step probably--

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:56.940
other guesses from other people.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:57.450
Its OK.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:57.949
Come on.

00:03:57.949 --> 00:04:00.830
Throw it out there.

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:01.360
One more.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:03.570
What do you do after
the first HR system?

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:05.520
OK, just try to
follow the code here,

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.280
and there is an account security
piece you typically have.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:10.740
Some sort of a username
password that's been generated,

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:13.800
like a temp password you've
got to sign in with it.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:15.630
And then there are
possibly some devices.

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:17.474
You may have a bring
your own strategy.

00:04:17.474 --> 00:04:19.140
You may have a
corporate-owned strategy.

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:19.740
What have you.

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:21.839
So you've got to think
about that dimension.

00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:23.422
And then you start
thinking about what

00:04:23.422 --> 00:04:26.070
cloud-based applications you're
going to assign or entitle

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:27.289
to that user.

00:04:27.289 --> 00:04:28.830
And then you think
through that flow.

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.746
So this is typically
what you're trying to do.

00:04:30.746 --> 00:04:32.400
The Identity Analytics
piece is really

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:35.770
trying to give you insights
into the usage patterns.

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:37.710
Maybe for a variety
of use cases.

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:39.307
Some of it may be
license management.

00:04:39.307 --> 00:04:40.890
You really want to
try and understand.

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:42.450
You bought all these licenses.

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:44.950
How is that actually being used
in your organization, right?

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:46.730
So that's possibly one thing.

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:49.080
There might be interesting
login patterns and alerts

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:50.454
that you may want
to think about.

00:04:50.454 --> 00:04:52.890
Maybe that's a security risk
that you want to get flagged.

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:57.060
So all of that falls under the
Identity Analytics umbrella.

00:04:57.060 --> 00:05:01.110
So we think about identity
in the context of these three

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:02.039
platform services.

00:05:02.039 --> 00:05:04.080
The first is the piece
that we just talked about,

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:05.280
which is directory.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:08.370
And this is the core where you
have your users, your groups,

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:10.620
the devices that you
need to get registered,

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:14.010
and the applications that those
users or devices have access

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:14.860
to.

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:17.070
So that's the first piece.

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:20.580
And we believe that cloud-based
identity is an extension

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:21.940
of your on-premise solution.

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:23.700
So it's really
important that there

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:27.660
are tools that help you sync
from, effectively, Microsoft

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:28.440
Active Directory.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:30.120
That's what you're using.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:31.440
As most of you are.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:33.360
And being able to
sync the existing

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:36.660
entities into the cloud
directory as well.

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:38.610
I think those tools
are important.

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:41.710
The next piece is around
access management.

00:05:41.710 --> 00:05:45.150
So it's really about thinking
from the end user perspective.

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:46.950
Let's think about the
single sign-on XP.

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:50.100
So if you have a Windows
device, you log in.

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:53.070
And your machine is domain
[INAUDIBLE], what have you.

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:54.750
You have Windows
Integrated Auth.

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:56.250
You authenticate
once, and you're

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:58.742
able to launch these
windows applications.

00:05:58.742 --> 00:06:00.700
But all that happens in
the cloud world, right?

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:05.490
And if an end user has to
have different passwords

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:08.700
for each application, they might
end up forgetting about it.

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:11.200
Or more likely-- this is
what end users do really,

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:12.130
really well--

00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:15.600
which is they come up with some
easy-to-remember password that

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:17.915
meets the minimum
security requirements,

00:06:17.915 --> 00:06:20.040
so that they can have to
type this again and again.

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:21.911
It's very easy for them to do.

00:06:21.911 --> 00:06:22.410
Right?

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:23.850
And instead if
you have something

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:26.940
like a single sign-on solution,
you authenticate once.

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:29.730
You can have strong password
requirements and strong auth

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:32.940
requirements, and still be able
to launch these applications.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:36.510
I remember there is an
employee that worked--

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:37.920
not for me, but with me.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:40.230
And he was very proud--

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:41.610
our company policy.

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:43.350
This is pre-Google.

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:45.210
He was very proud
of the fact that he

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:47.490
had detected that the
employee policy was

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:50.270
you couldn't use one of
the last 10 passwords,

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:51.610
when you change your password.

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:53.190
So he had a script.

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:57.240
And he would effectively
change your password 10 times

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:00.630
and then change it back to the
same password that you had.

00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:02.310
And he was a good
Samaritan, so he

00:07:02.310 --> 00:07:04.200
would walk around
socializing that script,

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:06.220
if anybody else
wanted to use it.

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:08.340
So passwords end up
getting in the way.

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:11.359
Users are always smarter
than your password policy.

00:07:11.359 --> 00:07:13.650
So it's really important that
we think about the access

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:14.550
management piece.

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:17.440
And there's also
policy and control.

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:20.785
So you might have a
sensitive payroll application

00:07:20.785 --> 00:07:22.910
that you may not want the
user to just authenticate

00:07:22.910 --> 00:07:24.690
with a password.

00:07:24.690 --> 00:07:26.734
So you may have static
policies which says, hey,

00:07:26.734 --> 00:07:29.150
if you're trying to access
this sensitive application-- it

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:31.290
may be an HR application,
what have you.

00:07:31.290 --> 00:07:32.910
May need a second factor auth.

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:35.040
You could also have
dynamic policies in them.

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:37.140
Because you might have
some run time evaluation.

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:39.914
Like maybe an IP boundary,
or some other context

00:07:39.914 --> 00:07:41.580
that needs to get
evaluated at run time.

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:45.100
All of that falls in the
access management bucket.

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:47.950
And last but not the least, as
people are moving to the cloud,

00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:51.006
or at least expanding and
extending to the cloud,

00:07:51.006 --> 00:07:52.630
we believe it's also
important to start

00:07:52.630 --> 00:07:56.710
thinking about standards and
be very standards-driven.

00:07:56.710 --> 00:07:59.710
When you think about
the on-premise model,

00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:02.197
there's lots of legacy
protocols that have been there.

00:08:02.197 --> 00:08:03.280
It creates vendor lock-in.

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:07.490
It creates these implementations
that become subpar over time.

00:08:07.490 --> 00:08:11.086
So if you are a big believer
in a standards-based approach--

00:08:11.086 --> 00:08:12.460
In fact, one of
the things I want

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:14.209
to talk about from a
[? pain ?] standpoint

00:08:14.209 --> 00:08:17.420
is this bucket on the top,
which is provisioning.

00:08:17.420 --> 00:08:19.240
It is overloaded
term in itself a bit.

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:21.290
In this context, it's
about admin provisioning.

00:08:21.290 --> 00:08:23.950
So when you think about
admin and the number of users

00:08:23.950 --> 00:08:26.770
that they have to
manage and the access

00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:28.800
of different applications
for those users.

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:30.880
So this starts to
become unwieldy,

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:34.510
because if you have half a
dozen to a dozen applications--

00:08:34.510 --> 00:08:35.620
cloud-based applications.

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:37.690
And you have to now
manage different users

00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:40.367
in each of these
applications, it becomes hard

00:08:40.367 --> 00:08:41.450
if you have to go do that.

00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:43.799
So as an example,
John has access

00:08:43.799 --> 00:08:47.680
in Work Day, In Sevice Now,
G Suite, Box, what have you.

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:49.940
And you keep managing that,
it becomes pretty hard.

00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:52.780
We think a good identity
solution can centralize that.

00:08:52.780 --> 00:08:56.170
So you have that one place.

00:08:56.170 --> 00:08:58.510
And the identity
vendor can actively

00:08:58.510 --> 00:09:02.200
manage the right user access
within those applications.

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:04.280
All driven by standards
like [? SKIM, ?] which

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:06.970
is specifically used
for the provision.

00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:10.690
OK, so that's sort of our
view of identity as a service.

00:09:10.690 --> 00:09:12.640
And we're very serious
about this thing.

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:17.950
And we realize as we've extended
and grown beyond G Suite--

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:20.890
I think G Suite was like the
first major offering that

00:09:20.890 --> 00:09:24.070
was targeted at businesses
and enterprises.

00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:27.130
And we realized that
there are customers

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:30.009
who are now onboarding
other Google services, GCP

00:09:30.009 --> 00:09:31.300
services that they're building.

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:33.560
Android Chrome, what have you.

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:36.220
And we are announcing
a standalone identity

00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:38.390
as a service solution.

00:09:38.390 --> 00:09:41.380
We are branding it
as Cloud Identity.

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:44.330
This is something that's
in development right now.

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:47.740
And we will have a bit
of this next quarter.

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:49.210
The key here is
that we really want

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:53.010
to make this super easy
for organizations as they

00:09:53.010 --> 00:09:55.150
onboard onto Google services.

00:09:55.150 --> 00:09:57.640
To onboard those
enterprise identity,

00:09:57.640 --> 00:10:00.140
using our identity as
a service solution.

00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:02.380
So again, the brand
is cloud identity.

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:07.720
It's in development, and we'll
have a beta next quarter.

00:10:07.720 --> 00:10:09.360
Here's the interesting part.

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:11.970
How many of you here
are G Suite users?

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:13.660
Again a quick show
of hands please.

00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:14.160
Very nice.

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:15.070
Thank you so much.

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:17.400
We appreciate you
being a G Suite user.

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:20.660
But here's the hidden
secret in all of this.

00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:24.720
Our Cloud Identity
solution is already a part

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:26.490
of that G Suite solution.

00:10:26.490 --> 00:10:28.680
So many of you,
if not all of you,

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:31.290
have been using our identity
solution to provision

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:34.560
and manage the variety
of applications that are

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:37.300
included as part of G Suite.

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:38.970
So the key thing that
we are doing here

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:42.350
is taking that identity
that was part of G Suite--

00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:44.286
so had to be a G Suite customer.

00:10:44.286 --> 00:10:45.910
And as people are
coming on and saying,

00:10:45.910 --> 00:10:48.840
hey, I'm trying to build
these GCP services.

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:51.400
I'm maybe not a G Suite
user, then what do I do?

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:52.734
I want to use your identity.

00:10:52.734 --> 00:10:54.400
So what we're doing
is pulling that out.

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:56.450
It's almost like this
best-kept secret.

00:10:56.450 --> 00:10:57.820
Shouldn't have been a secret.

00:10:57.820 --> 00:11:00.330
We are pulling that
out and making that

00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:02.550
a standalone identity
as a service solution.

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:04.290
Which we are calling
Cloud Identity.

00:11:04.290 --> 00:11:06.120
So here's the key thing.

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:08.540
This is not something that's
new that we are really

00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:09.594
using in beta.

00:11:09.594 --> 00:11:11.010
This is something
that's been used

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:12.660
by millions of
business customers

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:14.470
already as part of G Suite.

00:11:14.470 --> 00:11:17.280
So we are just unpacking it
a little bit, so you do not

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:20.360
have to be a G Suite user.

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:24.137
So going back, as we think
about enterprise identities,

00:11:24.137 --> 00:11:25.970
And we think about these
enterprise services

00:11:25.970 --> 00:11:29.540
that we bring to market that
are cloud identity offering,

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:31.820
you can now onboard those
identities as a first step.

00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:34.934
We know that identity is now
becoming front and center.

00:11:34.934 --> 00:11:36.350
[INAUDIBLE] the
heart and soul all

00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:38.570
of these additional
services that are

00:11:38.570 --> 00:11:40.190
being used in an organization.

00:11:40.190 --> 00:11:41.600
So the idea is you onboard once.

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:44.260
You might onboard--
maybe not with G Suite.

00:11:44.260 --> 00:11:47.060
You onboard maybe with a GCP
service you are building.

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:49.550
Maybe you're building an
application using our machine

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:51.560
learning services that
you want to roll out

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:53.300
for the subset of
your employees.

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:55.880
So you can do that with
Google Cloud Identity.

00:11:55.880 --> 00:11:57.480
And then at some
point in the future,

00:11:57.480 --> 00:11:59.480
you may want to try
out something else.

00:11:59.480 --> 00:12:02.570
You may want to try out Google
Drive for your organization.

00:12:02.570 --> 00:12:04.340
It's the same identity solution.

00:12:04.340 --> 00:12:06.260
You don't need to roll
out something new.

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:08.870
And it's all managed
through one admin control.

00:12:08.870 --> 00:12:14.210
So it's really easy to layer on
and add on additional services.

00:12:14.210 --> 00:12:15.980
OK, I hope that's appealing.

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:18.110
Here's the other piece
around Google Cloud.

00:12:18.110 --> 00:12:22.320
How many of you here are
existing GCP customers?

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:23.220
OK, good size.

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:24.630
And evaluating GCP?

00:12:24.630 --> 00:12:25.830
Maybe?

00:12:25.830 --> 00:12:26.970
OK.

00:12:26.970 --> 00:12:30.120
So when you think about our
GCP services and Google Cloud

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:31.860
Identity again-- in
fact, we'll walk you

00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:33.270
through a demo of this.

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:36.930
It's very easy for you to think
about this as a service aspect.

00:12:36.930 --> 00:12:40.140
So GCP already has a
variety of services for you.

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:43.350
You can now onboard and
sign up for cloud identity

00:12:43.350 --> 00:12:46.380
as another service, and it's
completely driven by being

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:49.670
self-service and online.

00:12:49.670 --> 00:12:51.710
The challenge for
those of you who

00:12:51.710 --> 00:12:54.740
are Google Cloud customers
is when to date--

00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:59.030
if you want enterprise identity,
it's an offline process.

00:12:59.030 --> 00:13:01.160
In the sense you typically
have to call a sales

00:13:01.160 --> 00:13:04.780
rep on the Google side, and
then they put in a request.

00:13:04.780 --> 00:13:07.490
We have to do some provisioning
magic at the back end.

00:13:07.490 --> 00:13:10.980
And then you get identity as
a service in your cloud admin

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:11.522
console.

00:13:11.522 --> 00:13:12.980
You no longer will
have to do that.

00:13:12.980 --> 00:13:17.310
And we'll show
that demo shortly.

00:13:17.310 --> 00:13:21.410
One last piece as we think about
Google and Google identity.

00:13:21.410 --> 00:13:24.560
Our Cloud Identity solution
also works beyond G Suite.

00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:28.970
So we do have use cases
when, even today, customers

00:13:28.970 --> 00:13:30.890
are not always using G Suite.

00:13:30.890 --> 00:13:34.010
Or definitely not for all the
employees in an organization.

00:13:34.010 --> 00:13:36.620
A classic example, we
have this large customer.

00:13:36.620 --> 00:13:39.680
They have a lot of
retail floor workers.

00:13:39.680 --> 00:13:42.380
So these are employees who
are using mobile devices.

00:13:42.380 --> 00:13:45.170
They have access to these
inventory applications built

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:47.930
on the cloud, but
not using G Suite.

00:13:47.930 --> 00:13:50.870
And they need access to
these inventory applications.

00:13:50.870 --> 00:13:53.630
And so they can
use Cloud Identity.

00:13:53.630 --> 00:13:56.360
So even though a subset of those
users are not using G Suite--

00:13:56.360 --> 00:13:57.380
others are--

00:13:57.380 --> 00:14:00.920
they use Cloud Identity in order
to manage other applications.

00:14:00.920 --> 00:14:02.420
Even if they're not G Suite.

00:14:02.420 --> 00:14:03.500
So that's the idea.

00:14:03.500 --> 00:14:07.190
We have out-of-the-box support
for things like single sign-on

00:14:07.190 --> 00:14:08.840
for a variety of applications.

00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:10.354
This is a small sample here.

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:14.830
OK.

00:14:14.830 --> 00:14:17.200
That is identity as a service.

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:20.140
That's Google's
identity as a service.

00:14:20.140 --> 00:14:21.640
And many of you
raised your hands

00:14:21.640 --> 00:14:23.690
and said you're already looking
at a cloud-based identity

00:14:23.690 --> 00:14:24.189
provider.

00:14:24.189 --> 00:14:26.150
So why worry about Google?

00:14:26.150 --> 00:14:27.460
Or why should you care?

00:14:27.460 --> 00:14:31.530
What's unique about what Google
has to offer in the space?

00:14:31.530 --> 00:14:32.940
The key here is one word.

00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:35.012
If you can remember
that one thing, is we

00:14:35.012 --> 00:14:36.720
are really, really
proud of our security.

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:40.200
You heard this in Diane's
keynote earlier today.

00:14:40.200 --> 00:14:44.340
And something that we take a lot
of pride in is around security.

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:45.761
So let's understand why.

00:14:45.761 --> 00:14:46.260
Right?

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:51.480
Let's start with the very
first thing about our services.

00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:53.250
We have seven
services that actually

00:14:53.250 --> 00:14:56.820
have a monthly active user
count of a billion users.

00:14:56.820 --> 00:14:57.580
OK.

00:14:57.580 --> 00:15:00.140
And I'll give you the first two.

00:15:00.140 --> 00:15:01.940
It's YouTube and Search.

00:15:01.940 --> 00:15:04.400
Anyone want to volunteer
the other names?

00:15:04.400 --> 00:15:08.352
What else has a monthly active
user count of a billion users?

00:15:08.352 --> 00:15:08.977
AUDIENCE: Apps.

00:15:08.977 --> 00:15:10.268
KARTHIK LAKSHMINARAYANAN: Apps.

00:15:10.268 --> 00:15:10.890
OK.

00:15:10.890 --> 00:15:11.796
Not all of apps.

00:15:11.796 --> 00:15:12.420
AUDIENCE: Maps.

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:12.780
KARTHIK LAKSHMINARAYANAN: Maps.

00:15:12.780 --> 00:15:13.020
Yes.

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:13.310
Maps.

00:15:13.310 --> 00:15:13.810
Correct.

00:15:13.810 --> 00:15:14.410
That's true.

00:15:14.410 --> 00:15:14.910
Others?

00:15:14.910 --> 00:15:15.680
AUDIENCE: Gmail.

00:15:15.680 --> 00:15:16.260
KARTHIK LAKSHMINARAYANAN: Gmail.

00:15:16.260 --> 00:15:16.885
That's correct.

00:15:16.885 --> 00:15:18.510
That's four.

00:15:18.510 --> 00:15:19.020
Other two.

00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:20.100
Maps and Gmail.

00:15:20.100 --> 00:15:21.280
AUDIENCE: Hangout.

00:15:21.280 --> 00:15:21.750
KARTHIK LAKSHMINARAYANAN:
Hangout.

00:15:21.750 --> 00:15:22.550
Not yet.

00:15:22.550 --> 00:15:24.470
We hope to get there.

00:15:24.470 --> 00:15:25.344
Sorry?

00:15:25.344 --> 00:15:26.260
AUDIENCE: Google Docs.

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:26.890
KARTHIK LAKSHMINARAYANAN:
Google Docs.

00:15:26.890 --> 00:15:27.560
Not yet.

00:15:27.560 --> 00:15:29.410
We'll get there.

00:15:29.410 --> 00:15:30.010
Drive.

00:15:30.010 --> 00:15:32.950
Also not there.

00:15:32.950 --> 00:15:33.532
Sorry?

00:15:33.532 --> 00:15:34.240
AUDIENCE: Chrome.

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:34.780
KARTHIK LAKSHMINARAYANAN:
Chrome, yes.

00:15:34.780 --> 00:15:35.260
That is correct.

00:15:35.260 --> 00:15:36.310
Chrome is the first one.

00:15:36.310 --> 00:15:40.914
So YouTube, Search,
Maps, Chrome, Gmail.

00:15:40.914 --> 00:15:41.740
AUDIENCE: Play.

00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:42.220
KARTHIK LAKSHMINARAYANAN: Play.

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:43.150
Number six.

00:15:43.150 --> 00:15:45.110
One more.

00:15:45.110 --> 00:15:48.420
Probably you carry
phones that run this.

00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:49.310
Android, thank you.

00:15:49.310 --> 00:15:50.310
That's seven.

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:52.940
So what happens when you
run these seven services--

00:15:52.940 --> 00:15:55.460
the more important
thing here is that we

00:15:55.460 --> 00:15:57.410
have sophisticated systems.

00:15:57.410 --> 00:15:59.060
These have all been
custom-built by us.

00:15:59.060 --> 00:16:01.277
And there are additional
talks on security

00:16:01.277 --> 00:16:02.360
throughout the conference.

00:16:02.360 --> 00:16:04.700
So we build these
custom-hardened systems

00:16:04.700 --> 00:16:07.790
that go all the way from
the hardware to the network

00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:09.644
to the software that
runs on it in order

00:16:09.644 --> 00:16:11.810
to ensure that they have
the right level of security

00:16:11.810 --> 00:16:12.770
and control.

00:16:12.770 --> 00:16:14.840
The other pieces that
come with it is we

00:16:14.840 --> 00:16:17.480
use machine-learning and
other sophisticated techniques

00:16:17.480 --> 00:16:20.180
to detect things like attacks.

00:16:20.180 --> 00:16:21.020
Spoofing attacks.

00:16:21.020 --> 00:16:21.890
Things like that.

00:16:21.890 --> 00:16:24.620
To keep this data really safe.

00:16:24.620 --> 00:16:26.290
And it's the same security.

00:16:26.290 --> 00:16:27.290
The same infrastructure.

00:16:27.290 --> 00:16:30.470
The same technology
that we are using that

00:16:30.470 --> 00:16:32.990
powers our cloud
identity as well.

00:16:32.990 --> 00:16:35.310
Here is the other key part
of it, which is reliability.

00:16:35.310 --> 00:16:38.480
So our business-- the money that
Google makes on an annual basis

00:16:38.480 --> 00:16:39.320
also runs on this.

00:16:39.320 --> 00:16:41.660
So if you can't, on
the consumer side,

00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:44.690
let's say log in and have
an Ads account that you're

00:16:44.690 --> 00:16:47.390
managing for your organization.

00:16:47.390 --> 00:16:49.880
That has a significant
impact to Google's business.

00:16:49.880 --> 00:16:52.940
So we go a long way to ensure
that this infrastructure is

00:16:52.940 --> 00:16:54.290
up and running.

00:16:54.290 --> 00:16:56.730
And protected against all
kinds of attacks and threats.

00:16:56.730 --> 00:16:59.750
So when you move your data--
those crown jewels that is now

00:16:59.750 --> 00:17:01.760
sitting in an
on-premise directory--

00:17:01.760 --> 00:17:04.578
and you extend that to
Google Cloud Identity,

00:17:04.578 --> 00:17:05.869
this is what's going to happen.

00:17:05.869 --> 00:17:08.960
The same security that we
use to protect our business

00:17:08.960 --> 00:17:13.088
is now going to go to work
protecting your business.

00:17:13.088 --> 00:17:14.629
So our security
becomes your security

00:17:14.629 --> 00:17:20.079
when you move to our
cloud identity solution.

00:17:20.079 --> 00:17:21.730
Let's talk about one
more thing, which

00:17:21.730 --> 00:17:25.030
is around account security.

00:17:25.030 --> 00:17:27.869
How many of you here are using
some form of second factor

00:17:27.869 --> 00:17:29.950
auth in your organization?

00:17:29.950 --> 00:17:33.040
Anyone not using
second factor auth?

00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:34.270
OK.

00:17:34.270 --> 00:17:37.000
I think everyone's probably
moved on from not just having

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:39.160
a password-based control.

00:17:39.160 --> 00:17:43.210
And when we think about
one-time-use passwords,

00:17:43.210 --> 00:17:46.600
the most common ones end
up being some sort of phone

00:17:46.600 --> 00:17:49.880
that people are using to
get this one-time-use key.

00:17:49.880 --> 00:17:52.950
Do you see a few different
problems that come up with it?

00:17:52.950 --> 00:17:54.380
The first being that--

00:17:54.380 --> 00:17:57.000
and probably the most important
one is being around cost.

00:17:57.000 --> 00:17:59.640
Because when you enable a
second factor auth solution that

00:17:59.640 --> 00:18:02.080
focuses on a mobile
phone, the employee

00:18:02.080 --> 00:18:06.210
is asking the organization
to pay for that bill.

00:18:06.210 --> 00:18:07.641
And that increases the costs.

00:18:07.641 --> 00:18:09.390
If you forget your
phone or what have you,

00:18:09.390 --> 00:18:11.130
then you now have
this weird thing

00:18:11.130 --> 00:18:12.549
of how do I log in, et cetera.

00:18:12.549 --> 00:18:13.840
So there's a problem with that.

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:16.780
There are other issues, like
SMS connectivity and so on.

00:18:16.780 --> 00:18:19.050
But probably from a
usability standpoint,

00:18:19.050 --> 00:18:20.550
the thing that
happens is every time

00:18:20.550 --> 00:18:22.380
you go to type in this
key-- whether it's

00:18:22.380 --> 00:18:25.290
from a VPN-based,
one of those keys,

00:18:25.290 --> 00:18:28.220
or whether it is from
your one-time password.

00:18:28.220 --> 00:18:30.750
And Verizon had this really
interesting data point,

00:18:30.750 --> 00:18:32.130
where they did this survey.

00:18:32.130 --> 00:18:34.560
And they found that--

00:18:34.560 --> 00:18:37.320
I think it was about 75%, 79%.

00:18:37.320 --> 00:18:38.340
Something like that.

00:18:38.340 --> 00:18:39.750
A really high number.

00:18:39.750 --> 00:18:43.110
That the attacks actually
happened because existing

00:18:43.110 --> 00:18:45.150
accounts-- the
existing employees,

00:18:45.150 --> 00:18:46.727
their accounts get compromised.

00:18:46.727 --> 00:18:48.060
And then that's how they get in.

00:18:48.060 --> 00:18:49.560
So it's not really
some hacker who's

00:18:49.560 --> 00:18:51.240
able to break through
into your system,

00:18:51.240 --> 00:18:53.130
it's an employee
that gets phished,

00:18:53.130 --> 00:18:56.400
and then it's that employee's
account masquerading around,

00:18:56.400 --> 00:18:59.080
causing all kind of
havoc in your system.

00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:00.670
So there's a
fundamental problem,

00:19:00.670 --> 00:19:02.045
because when you
get a key that's

00:19:02.045 --> 00:19:05.340
valid for one, five minutes,
that key can be read.

00:19:05.340 --> 00:19:07.710
It's human-readable,
and it can be phished.

00:19:07.710 --> 00:19:08.990
So that's a key problem.

00:19:08.990 --> 00:19:11.140
So something that
Google worked on--

00:19:11.140 --> 00:19:12.630
working with the FIDO Alliance.

00:19:12.630 --> 00:19:15.480
And again, contributing
back to that open standards

00:19:15.480 --> 00:19:18.250
and open source
community is this thing

00:19:18.250 --> 00:19:20.490
which you call security keys.

00:19:20.490 --> 00:19:22.770
And it's something
that you may have

00:19:22.770 --> 00:19:25.020
heard of if you are a G
Suite enterprise customer.

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:26.430
It's included in that.

00:19:26.430 --> 00:19:28.964
But these security
keys are interesting.

00:19:28.964 --> 00:19:30.630
All of you are going
to get one of these

00:19:30.630 --> 00:19:33.740
when you walk out today.

00:19:33.740 --> 00:19:36.240
This is something that
has a couple of ways

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:37.650
you put it into a USB key.

00:19:37.650 --> 00:19:39.750
Like those two keys on
the left and the right.

00:19:39.750 --> 00:19:43.050
And then the one in the middle
is a Bluetooth low energy

00:19:43.050 --> 00:19:46.390
enabled key, so it works
with your phone as well.

00:19:46.390 --> 00:19:47.820
Here's a key
difference, OK, which

00:19:47.820 --> 00:19:49.660
is it's an encrypted signal.

00:19:49.660 --> 00:19:50.820
So even if you are phished.

00:19:50.820 --> 00:19:53.100
Instead of going
to Google dot com,

00:19:53.100 --> 00:19:54.990
you went to goggle dot com.

00:19:54.990 --> 00:19:57.390
And you are typing
your password in.

00:19:57.390 --> 00:19:59.520
Even if somebody
gets that password,

00:19:59.520 --> 00:20:01.190
your signal is
completely encrypted.

00:20:01.190 --> 00:20:02.670
So the second factor--

00:20:02.670 --> 00:20:05.339
So that person who
phished you is not

00:20:05.339 --> 00:20:06.630
going to do much with that key.

00:20:06.630 --> 00:20:09.730
Because Google is going to look
for, not just the password,

00:20:09.730 --> 00:20:12.900
but the whole second key that's
sent through an encrypted

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:13.680
signal.

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:15.490
And that token is not
going to be valid.

00:20:15.490 --> 00:20:20.220
And this auth attempt
is going to be declined.

00:20:20.220 --> 00:20:23.380
In fact, interesting story--
that I have been at Google,

00:20:23.380 --> 00:20:26.750
and I have never changed
my actual password.

00:20:26.750 --> 00:20:29.760
And I swear to god, on my
first day I had to change it,

00:20:29.760 --> 00:20:31.290
based on the temp
password I got.

00:20:31.290 --> 00:20:33.090
I've never changed my password.

00:20:33.090 --> 00:20:34.770
I do not VPN in from anywhere.

00:20:34.770 --> 00:20:37.050
I can work from a
kiosk like this.

00:20:37.050 --> 00:20:40.260
And I'm a director at
Google, so I get access

00:20:40.260 --> 00:20:42.000
to a fair amount
of sensitive data.

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:44.444
Employee data, perf
data, things like that.

00:20:44.444 --> 00:20:46.110
And I'm not asked to
change my password,

00:20:46.110 --> 00:20:49.110
because every time I log
in I have the security key.

00:20:49.110 --> 00:20:52.680
So Google runs our
business on this security,

00:20:52.680 --> 00:20:54.722
and these keys are
now available for you.

00:20:54.722 --> 00:20:57.180
Each and every one of you is
going to get one of these keys

00:20:57.180 --> 00:20:58.920
when you walk out later today.

00:20:58.920 --> 00:20:59.910
Please do try it out.

00:20:59.910 --> 00:21:04.050
If you like the experience,
continue rolling this out more

00:21:04.050 --> 00:21:06.950
broadly in your organization.

00:21:06.950 --> 00:21:09.190
[INAUDIBLE] account security.

00:21:09.190 --> 00:21:13.277
One more thing is that
these keys are available.

