00:30:49.330 --> 00:30:50.800
for your organization.

00:30:50.800 --> 00:30:54.820
And it's something, again, it's
in beta early next quarter.

00:30:54.820 --> 00:30:57.340
And we hope that
you like using it.

00:30:57.340 --> 00:31:00.370
And this is not tied to G
Suite or anything like that.

00:31:00.370 --> 00:31:05.020
You can just sign up for
Google's Cloud Identity.

00:31:05.020 --> 00:31:07.680
So let's talk a little
bit about go to markets.

00:31:07.680 --> 00:31:11.090
Recall, again, from a demo
and from an agenda standpoint,

00:31:11.090 --> 00:31:13.600
we wanted to talk about, what
is Identity as a Service?

00:31:13.600 --> 00:31:15.610
What's Google's
solution in this space?

00:31:15.610 --> 00:31:17.110
We talked about our
differentiators,

00:31:17.110 --> 00:31:18.370
which is security.

00:31:18.370 --> 00:31:21.099
And now we want to talk about
how you think about this

00:31:21.099 --> 00:31:22.390
from a go-to-market standpoint.

00:31:22.390 --> 00:31:24.264
Because like we were
saying at the beginning,

00:31:24.264 --> 00:31:25.630
there is an on-premise world.

00:31:25.630 --> 00:31:28.510
And this is that well-managed,
well-oiled machine

00:31:28.510 --> 00:31:29.500
that you're running.

00:31:29.500 --> 00:31:32.380
Now you're extending it
and bringing cloud identity

00:31:32.380 --> 00:31:33.370
into your organization.

00:31:33.370 --> 00:31:36.070
So as you start to do that,
it's important to think

00:31:36.070 --> 00:31:38.080
about these hybrid use cases.

00:31:38.080 --> 00:31:40.240
And what Google
does is we support

00:31:40.240 --> 00:31:42.760
these web standards and
[? modern ?] standards

00:31:42.760 --> 00:31:45.280
on SAML and OAuth and OIDC.

00:31:45.280 --> 00:31:46.950
What we don't have
out of the box

00:31:46.950 --> 00:31:51.720
is support for these legacy
or identity 1.0 protocols,

00:31:51.720 --> 00:31:54.350
like Kerberos and WS Fed.

00:31:54.350 --> 00:31:57.930
But this is where we
have partnered closely.

00:31:57.930 --> 00:32:00.790
We have these two
partners, Ping and Okta.

00:32:00.790 --> 00:32:03.940
And we have them as part of our
recommended for work program.

00:32:03.940 --> 00:32:07.900
And the idea being that you use
these partners as an ID bridge.

00:32:07.900 --> 00:32:10.480
So the idea being we
could authenticate,

00:32:10.480 --> 00:32:12.460
treat Ping and Okta as an app.

00:32:12.460 --> 00:32:16.660
And that, in turn, takes
care of your existing cloud

00:32:16.660 --> 00:32:18.370
on-premise use cases.

00:32:18.370 --> 00:32:21.620
So we can do the cloud use cases
and the existing on-premise use

00:32:21.620 --> 00:32:22.120
cases.

00:32:22.120 --> 00:32:25.510
You can partner with somebody
like a Ping and Okta.

00:32:25.510 --> 00:32:28.350
And we have a great
relationship with them.

00:32:28.350 --> 00:32:30.450
So very quickly,
why consider this?

00:32:30.450 --> 00:32:33.930
Because really, it's a get
the best of both worlds

00:32:33.930 --> 00:32:35.070
kind of scenario, right?

00:32:35.070 --> 00:32:40.050
We think that if you believe in
our value prop and our promise

00:32:40.050 --> 00:32:42.210
that our security
is going to do more

00:32:42.210 --> 00:32:44.610
to protect your
directory in the cloud,

00:32:44.610 --> 00:32:46.680
then please go with
us as that cloud

00:32:46.680 --> 00:32:48.960
identity as your directory.

00:32:48.960 --> 00:32:51.060
And use that to
protect your crown

00:32:51.060 --> 00:32:53.280
jewels for those
cloud-based use cases.

00:32:53.280 --> 00:32:56.790
And use the partners
as an ID bridge

00:32:56.790 --> 00:32:59.719
in order to address some of
your more complex use cases

00:32:59.719 --> 00:33:01.135
and some of your
hybrid use cases.

00:33:05.250 --> 00:33:07.360
Don't just take my word for it.

00:33:07.360 --> 00:33:10.260
We actually have an
additional speaker

00:33:10.260 --> 00:33:11.790
that we didn't use upfront.

00:33:11.790 --> 00:33:13.950
And this is from Netflix.

00:33:13.950 --> 00:33:18.160
And it's my pleasure to
introduce Tejas Dharamshi.

00:33:18.160 --> 00:33:20.100
He is a senior security
software engineer.

00:33:20.100 --> 00:33:22.980
He works on Netflix
cloud security.

00:33:22.980 --> 00:33:25.050
So please learn more about--

00:33:25.050 --> 00:33:27.780
please share more with
us about how you're

00:33:27.780 --> 00:33:29.290
using Google Cloud Identity.

00:33:29.290 --> 00:33:30.750
And I think it's
fascinating to see

00:33:30.750 --> 00:33:35.190
how a forward-thinking
customer is using us to serve

00:33:35.190 --> 00:33:36.920
their mission critical needs.

00:33:36.920 --> 00:33:39.706
But to hear more about
it, please welcome Tejas.

00:33:39.706 --> 00:33:40.830
TEJAS DHARAMSHI: Thank you.

00:33:40.830 --> 00:33:42.621
KARTHIK LAKSHMINARAYANAN:
Thank you, Tejas.

00:33:44.499 --> 00:33:45.540
TEJAS DHARAMSHI: So, yes.

00:33:45.540 --> 00:33:49.260
Before we get into learning what
identity and how Netflix deals

00:33:49.260 --> 00:33:51.510
with identity, let's
first understand

00:33:51.510 --> 00:33:55.610
what it means to be in
a Netflix ecosystem.

00:33:55.610 --> 00:33:58.830
So we have apps,
studios apps, basically,

00:33:58.830 --> 00:34:02.370
which manage the entire
life cycle of a production,

00:34:02.370 --> 00:34:06.390
from an inception to PR to
marketing to localization

00:34:06.390 --> 00:34:07.500
to encoding [INAUDIBLE].

00:34:07.500 --> 00:34:09.540
So entire end-to-end
workflow, we

00:34:09.540 --> 00:34:13.027
have a suite of
applications to do that.

00:34:13.027 --> 00:34:14.610
Then we have engineering
applications,

00:34:14.610 --> 00:34:16.770
which manage the
entire deployment

00:34:16.770 --> 00:34:19.630
pipeline to our
big data platform

00:34:19.630 --> 00:34:22.139
to the two other
developer tools.

00:34:22.139 --> 00:34:24.780
So a whole bunch of
engineering apps.

00:34:24.780 --> 00:34:27.790
And we have corporate
applications.

00:34:27.790 --> 00:34:32.480
So we're talking about more
than 500 such applications.

00:34:32.480 --> 00:34:36.320
So in such varied apps,
what you don't want

00:34:36.320 --> 00:34:39.350
is everyone going and
building their own identity

00:34:39.350 --> 00:34:42.320
directory and managing
authentication, session

00:34:42.320 --> 00:34:46.280
management, and other aspects.

00:34:46.280 --> 00:34:49.489
It's a huge risk, especially
for a firm like Netflix.

00:34:49.489 --> 00:34:51.830
So we definitely
don't want to do this.

00:34:51.830 --> 00:34:55.150
What we want is a single
identity directory.

00:34:55.150 --> 00:34:57.260
This is where Cloud Identity
offering from Google

00:34:57.260 --> 00:34:59.210
comes into picture.

00:34:59.210 --> 00:35:03.080
And at the same time, don't
confuse people, right?

00:35:03.080 --> 00:35:05.345
Don't confuse with
varied login experience.

00:35:05.345 --> 00:35:08.090
Just a single login experience.

00:35:08.090 --> 00:35:10.715
That's another key aspect.

00:35:10.715 --> 00:35:12.590
And also, we don't want
everyone to just keep

00:35:12.590 --> 00:35:14.881
logging in all the time to
access so many applications.

00:35:14.881 --> 00:35:17.990
We want single sign-on.

00:35:17.990 --> 00:35:24.020
And the last piece, which is
key to us, is adaptive security.

00:35:24.020 --> 00:35:27.470
How can we extend
the security model

00:35:27.470 --> 00:35:29.990
and add Netflix juice
in there, right?

00:35:29.990 --> 00:35:34.840
So adaptive security
is another key aspect.

00:35:34.840 --> 00:35:39.490
So this leads to the
birth of Netflix identity.

00:35:39.490 --> 00:35:40.690
We call it Meechum.

00:35:40.690 --> 00:35:43.120
I hope everyone is aware
of the character Meechum

00:35:43.120 --> 00:35:44.305
from "House of Cards."

00:35:44.305 --> 00:35:47.810
So that's where the
name comes from.

00:35:47.810 --> 00:35:53.110
So the heart to our identity
is our infrastructure.

00:35:53.110 --> 00:35:56.470
So we have a highly scalable,
active/active, multi-region

00:35:56.470 --> 00:35:58.600
infrastructure on AWS.

00:35:58.600 --> 00:36:03.226
That's where our identity
infrastructure runs off.

00:36:03.226 --> 00:36:05.100
As Karthik was mentioning,
and on the slides,

00:36:05.100 --> 00:36:08.040
we use Google as the
identity directory

00:36:08.040 --> 00:36:13.910
as well as our provider,
and we use Ping as a broker.

00:36:13.910 --> 00:36:16.510
Ping actually helps us
add that last piece,

00:36:16.510 --> 00:36:20.637
which we're talking about, as
the custom adaptive security

00:36:20.637 --> 00:36:21.970
that we're talking about, right?

00:36:21.970 --> 00:36:24.420
So I'm going to go more
in detail about that.

00:36:24.420 --> 00:36:30.060
But these are the core aspects
about Netflix identity.

00:36:30.060 --> 00:36:33.150
So the Cloud Identity
offering from Google,

00:36:33.150 --> 00:36:35.010
it provide some key aspects.

00:36:35.010 --> 00:36:37.350
A, custom user schema.

00:36:37.350 --> 00:36:39.240
So based on your
organization requirements,

00:36:39.240 --> 00:36:42.780
you can define your
own custom schema

00:36:42.780 --> 00:36:46.720
and mark attributes
that are public,

00:36:46.720 --> 00:36:50.880
which means that anyone can
access, or admin attributes.

00:36:50.880 --> 00:36:52.380
In this particular
case, I'm talking

00:36:52.380 --> 00:36:56.610
about the hire date
or employee type

00:36:56.610 --> 00:37:01.110
or any other
sensitive information.

00:37:01.110 --> 00:37:03.180
And the second thing
that it actually helps us

00:37:03.180 --> 00:37:05.940
is automate the entire
user life cycle.

00:37:05.940 --> 00:37:10.080
So [INAUDIBLE] gives you
APIs to actually do that.

00:37:10.080 --> 00:37:13.260
So in our case, user
gets created in Workday,

00:37:13.260 --> 00:37:20.460
gets synced to the ID
identity directory, and even

00:37:20.460 --> 00:37:22.750
the deprovisioning aspect.

00:37:22.750 --> 00:37:24.750
Next aspect is groups.

00:37:24.750 --> 00:37:29.320
So the way we treat
groups at Netflix

00:37:29.320 --> 00:37:34.060
is like, we use groups as a way
of [INAUDIBLE] applications.

00:37:34.060 --> 00:37:37.240
Either developers
use groups as a way

00:37:37.240 --> 00:37:39.550
to [INAUDIBLE] in their
application or admin

00:37:39.550 --> 00:37:44.350
created groups to [INAUDIBLE]
very sensitive applications.

00:37:44.350 --> 00:37:49.700
Another aspect that the Cloud
Identity offering provides us

00:37:49.700 --> 00:37:55.510
is basically the support
of isolation, right?

00:37:55.510 --> 00:38:00.910
So you can isolate people and
groups in their own domains.

00:38:00.910 --> 00:38:02.200
You can create subdomains.

00:38:02.200 --> 00:38:03.940
But the way we want
it to go about that

00:38:03.940 --> 00:38:08.040
is to have their own
distinct domains.

00:38:08.040 --> 00:38:10.760
So in our case, we have
employees in their own domain.

00:38:10.760 --> 00:38:13.430
Contractors, customer
service, DBD,

00:38:13.430 --> 00:38:16.360
everyone in their own domains.

00:38:16.360 --> 00:38:19.142
And the OpenID Connect
User info endpoint

00:38:19.142 --> 00:38:20.350
can be customized now, right?

00:38:20.350 --> 00:38:22.840
So you can create your
own user information

00:38:22.840 --> 00:38:25.380
that you want to expose
as part of the OpenID

00:38:25.380 --> 00:38:28.480
Connect [INAUDIBLE].

00:38:28.480 --> 00:38:32.260
So let's take the classic
example of an OpenID Connect

00:38:32.260 --> 00:38:33.840
authentication workflow.

00:38:33.840 --> 00:38:36.834
A user goes through one of
these engineering applications.

00:38:36.834 --> 00:38:38.500
They don't have a
valid Meechum session,

00:38:38.500 --> 00:38:40.570
they get redirected to Meechum.

00:38:40.570 --> 00:38:42.970
Further down, there's
no valid Google session,

00:38:42.970 --> 00:38:44.476
gets redirected to Google.

00:38:44.476 --> 00:38:46.600
And that's where the unified
login experience comes

00:38:46.600 --> 00:38:47.950
into picture, right?

00:38:47.950 --> 00:38:51.040
In addition to that, based
on risk analysis carried out

00:38:51.040 --> 00:38:55.110
by Google, you might be
presented by the second factor.

00:38:55.110 --> 00:38:59.940
On everything successful, an
access token and an ID token

00:38:59.940 --> 00:39:01.180
gets generated.

00:39:01.180 --> 00:39:05.170
Classic authentication cycle.

00:39:05.170 --> 00:39:09.070
And that's the first piece.

00:39:09.070 --> 00:39:13.930
Unsuccessful authentication,
ask and query user information.

00:39:13.930 --> 00:39:17.659
And do [INAUDIBLE] around
that or do some custom stuff

00:39:17.659 --> 00:39:18.450
around that, right?

00:39:18.450 --> 00:39:21.190
So do sample user,
info, endpoint,

00:39:21.190 --> 00:39:24.550
exposing public attributes
as well as the groups

00:39:24.550 --> 00:39:27.220
that I'm part of.

00:39:27.220 --> 00:39:29.530
So now this helps us
check three boxes, right?

00:39:29.530 --> 00:39:32.260
So now we have Google or
the Cloud Identity offering

00:39:32.260 --> 00:39:36.520
being the central ID or
the identity directory.

00:39:36.520 --> 00:39:38.710
We have unified
login experience.

00:39:38.710 --> 00:39:40.930
And we have added
single sign-on.

00:39:40.930 --> 00:39:43.870
So does that mean
that we call it a day

00:39:43.870 --> 00:39:46.360
and we are happy with that?

00:39:46.360 --> 00:39:49.520
How can we take this
to the next step?

00:39:49.520 --> 00:39:51.629
And this is where the
adaptive authentication

00:39:51.629 --> 00:39:52.420
comes into picture.

00:40:09.112 --> 00:40:13.720
So me as a user, can I
access pretty much any app

00:40:13.720 --> 00:40:17.020
that we have in a
suite of applications?

00:40:17.020 --> 00:40:20.076
Probably not a good idea, right?

00:40:20.076 --> 00:40:21.700
So that's where
adaptive authentication

00:40:21.700 --> 00:40:24.010
comes into picture.

00:40:24.010 --> 00:40:28.550
And the key factors to that
are role-based access control,

00:40:28.550 --> 00:40:30.760
what sort of a pattern,
access pattern,

00:40:30.760 --> 00:40:33.420
me as a user has for
these [INAUDIBLE]

00:40:33.420 --> 00:40:37.040
set of applications, and
finally, anomaly detection.

00:40:37.040 --> 00:40:39.290
I'm going to dive into each
of these aspects in a bit.

00:40:39.290 --> 00:40:41.530
But these are the
core constructs

00:40:41.530 --> 00:40:44.320
around our adaptive
authentication.

00:40:44.320 --> 00:40:46.970
So the first one is
role-based access control.

00:40:46.970 --> 00:40:50.800
So now we have every user
in their own Google domain.

00:40:50.800 --> 00:40:53.480
We have users and
we have groups.

00:40:53.480 --> 00:40:57.460
And remember, user meaning they
had their own custom schema.

00:40:57.460 --> 00:40:58.860
So how can we leverage that?

00:40:58.860 --> 00:41:01.850
So these attributes
are available.

00:41:01.850 --> 00:41:04.240
And they are available
to every developer who is

00:41:04.240 --> 00:41:06.370
building an app within Netflix.

00:41:06.370 --> 00:41:08.970
And we believe in freedom
and responsibility.

00:41:08.970 --> 00:41:11.290
So these attributes
are available to them.

00:41:11.290 --> 00:41:14.140
And they use self-service
tools to go and say

00:41:14.140 --> 00:41:16.630
that oh, I want to
enable all Netflix

00:41:16.630 --> 00:41:19.420
employees for my
application or contractors.

00:41:19.420 --> 00:41:22.410
And further down, I can say that
which groups and users I want

00:41:22.410 --> 00:41:24.250
to allow access to.

00:41:24.250 --> 00:41:28.300
The next step to this is,
also, can we use a schema

00:41:28.300 --> 00:41:31.480
and say that, hey, everyone in
this organization has access?

00:41:31.480 --> 00:41:33.790
So that I don't have to worry
about new people joining

00:41:33.790 --> 00:41:34.990
or people leaving.

00:41:34.990 --> 00:41:36.859
Everyone will have access.

00:41:36.859 --> 00:41:37.900
So that's the first step.

00:41:41.050 --> 00:41:45.070
The next step is
access pattern, right?

00:41:45.070 --> 00:41:49.760
So we have users now who
have access based on roles.

00:41:49.760 --> 00:41:53.840
But my account
can be compromised

00:41:53.840 --> 00:41:55.940
or my device gets stolen.

00:41:55.940 --> 00:41:57.540
There can be varied scenarios.

00:41:57.540 --> 00:41:59.960
So we need to figure
out what is normal

00:41:59.960 --> 00:42:03.440
and what is not normal from
a user's perspective, right?

00:42:03.440 --> 00:42:06.225
So [? page ?] [? is ?] accessing
an engineering application

00:42:06.225 --> 00:42:08.600
versus [? page is ?] all of
a sudden going to a financial

00:42:08.600 --> 00:42:11.510
application or to a production
suite of applications.

00:42:11.510 --> 00:42:12.990
What is normal?

00:42:12.990 --> 00:42:17.100
So from Meechum,
all this information

00:42:17.100 --> 00:42:19.960
gets streamed and logging
into our centralized logging

00:42:19.960 --> 00:42:21.790
infrastructure.

00:42:21.790 --> 00:42:26.860
From there, data aggregation
happens and a user profile

00:42:26.860 --> 00:42:30.850
is built. So user
profile meaning

00:42:30.850 --> 00:42:32.530
from where the
user is accessing,

00:42:32.530 --> 00:42:35.120
which app user is accessing.

00:42:35.120 --> 00:42:36.580
When is he accessing?

00:42:36.580 --> 00:42:40.060
At what frequency is the user
accessing the application?

00:42:40.060 --> 00:42:44.090
So based on that, a user
profile is basically built.

00:42:44.090 --> 00:42:48.326
Now we have the profile,
how do we define policies?

00:42:48.326 --> 00:42:49.700
Again, we have
self-service tools

00:42:49.700 --> 00:42:53.840
to create a policy for
every app, for every user,

00:42:53.840 --> 00:42:56.600
and define a whole bunch
of metrics around that.

00:42:56.600 --> 00:43:01.414
So now we have policy and
we have a user profile.

00:43:01.414 --> 00:43:03.080
How can we gel these
two things together

00:43:03.080 --> 00:43:04.230
during the authentication?

00:43:04.230 --> 00:43:07.280
So in Meechum, we
have another adapter

00:43:07.280 --> 00:43:10.970
that kicks in, which is an
access pattern adapter, which

00:43:10.970 --> 00:43:14.270
takes a peek into these
user profile and policies.

00:43:14.270 --> 00:43:16.920
And if something
unusual is detected,

00:43:16.920 --> 00:43:20.690
a second factor is sent to
the end user using [? dual. ?]

00:43:20.690 --> 00:43:25.410
And only after confirmation
is a user allowed access.

00:43:25.410 --> 00:43:29.660
So this is where, in addition
to Google's second factor, based

00:43:29.660 --> 00:43:34.130
on our risk analysis, we send
out a second factor to the end

00:43:34.130 --> 00:43:36.980
user using [? dual ?] security.

00:43:36.980 --> 00:43:40.430
And the last piece
being anomaly detection.

00:43:40.430 --> 00:43:43.220
Again, from the
information that is getting

00:43:43.220 --> 00:43:48.080
logged into our
logging structure,

00:43:48.080 --> 00:43:52.580
we figure out whether
things are normal

00:43:52.580 --> 00:43:57.230
or there's any anomaly
in your access pattern.

00:43:57.230 --> 00:44:00.260
Maybe you are trying to access
the same app at the same time

00:44:00.260 --> 00:44:01.220
from multiple devices.

00:44:01.220 --> 00:44:02.460
Is that really normal?

00:44:02.460 --> 00:44:03.710
May not be, right?

00:44:03.710 --> 00:44:08.150
So in that case, an email
is sent out to the end user.

00:44:08.150 --> 00:44:10.340
Only after confirmation
on that email

00:44:10.340 --> 00:44:14.210
will the access be
granted to that end user.

00:44:14.210 --> 00:44:17.720
So this is what is
Netflix identity

00:44:17.720 --> 00:44:20.750
and, in general,
adaptive authentication.

00:44:20.750 --> 00:44:25.420
And now we have all
four checkboxes checked.

00:44:25.420 --> 00:44:27.520
So this is what it means.

00:44:27.520 --> 00:44:30.540
So just to summarize
the four pillars,

00:44:30.540 --> 00:44:33.225
AWS is where our
entire infrastructure

00:44:33.225 --> 00:44:37.380
is, Google being the primary
identity directory as well

00:44:37.380 --> 00:44:38.640
as our provider.

00:44:38.640 --> 00:44:41.100
And Ping being our ID
broker, which helps us

00:44:41.100 --> 00:44:44.614
get to adaptive authentication.

00:44:44.614 --> 00:44:48.030
Thank you.

00:44:48.030 --> 00:44:50.357
Thanks, Karthik.

00:44:50.357 --> 00:44:52.190
KARTHIK LAKSHMINARAYANAN:
Thank you so much.

00:44:52.190 --> 00:44:56.550
We thought it'll be helpful to
just forget all the marketing

00:44:56.550 --> 00:44:57.465
and talk high level.

00:44:57.465 --> 00:44:59.256
We thought it'd be
helpful to just walk you

00:44:59.256 --> 00:45:01.740
through an actual customer,
how they're using us

00:45:01.740 --> 00:45:04.920
as part of their
critical infrastructure

00:45:04.920 --> 00:45:06.520
and as part of
their productivity

00:45:06.520 --> 00:45:08.825
in day-to-day business.

00:45:08.825 --> 00:45:10.950
I think the other piece,
just to quickly reiterate,

00:45:10.950 --> 00:45:14.070
I think Netflix has been
a great G Suite customer.

00:45:14.070 --> 00:45:17.180
And that's how they've been
able to run Cloud Identity.

00:45:17.180 --> 00:45:19.050
And just to reiterate,
now we'll have

00:45:19.050 --> 00:45:20.280
this as a standalone service.

00:45:20.280 --> 00:45:23.930
That will be the beta
that'll go out next quarter.

00:45:23.930 --> 00:45:25.440
So thanks again.

00:45:25.440 --> 00:45:27.430
We hope that that was useful.

00:45:27.430 --> 00:45:29.730
So we are close to the end.

00:45:29.730 --> 00:45:32.020
We talked about our
identity solution.

00:45:32.020 --> 00:45:34.080
We talked about our
differentiators.

00:45:34.080 --> 00:45:37.290
We talked about how we could
go to market with partners.

00:45:37.290 --> 00:45:40.500
And then the Netflix case study.

00:45:40.500 --> 00:45:43.620
One thing I do want to bring up,
it's near and dear to my heart.

00:45:43.620 --> 00:45:46.650
As I talk to customers
and as I talk to people,

00:45:46.650 --> 00:45:48.720
the question that always
comes up is people do

00:45:48.720 --> 00:45:51.070
think Google's tech
is pretty good.

00:45:51.070 --> 00:45:52.080
That's not the issue.

00:45:52.080 --> 00:45:55.200
People do worry about,
what does it mean when

00:45:55.200 --> 00:45:59.760
my stuff is running on Google?

00:45:59.760 --> 00:46:01.220
And they always
worry, whether it

00:46:01.220 --> 00:46:03.500
is because of [INAUDIBLE]
created by the competition

00:46:03.500 --> 00:46:05.490
or whether it's real
concerns, we acknowledge

00:46:05.490 --> 00:46:07.410
that perception is reality.

00:46:07.410 --> 00:46:09.690
The fact that they worry,
is this data something

00:46:09.690 --> 00:46:12.540
that Google is now going
to mine, start showing ads

00:46:12.540 --> 00:46:13.980
around it?

00:46:13.980 --> 00:46:16.020
Absolutely not the case, right?

00:46:16.020 --> 00:46:17.520
Please do read our
terms of service.

00:46:17.520 --> 00:46:19.980
Our terms of service
for enterprise use cases

00:46:19.980 --> 00:46:21.030
is different.

00:46:21.030 --> 00:46:24.630
And we have different
thoughts for consumer.

00:46:24.630 --> 00:46:26.190
Your data is your data, right?

00:46:26.190 --> 00:46:27.630
We are not here
to show ads on it.

00:46:27.630 --> 00:46:29.050
So please, that is not the case.

00:46:29.050 --> 00:46:32.440
Please read terms of service and
you'll see exactly how we plan

00:46:32.440 --> 00:46:34.720
or how we use the data or not.

00:46:34.720 --> 00:46:36.220
So that's the first thing.

00:46:36.220 --> 00:46:36.845
No ads.

00:46:36.845 --> 00:46:38.220
If you had to
remember one thing,

00:46:38.220 --> 00:46:39.870
no, we don't use it for ads, OK?

00:46:39.870 --> 00:46:40.800
So that's one.

00:46:40.800 --> 00:46:43.540
The second is about compliance
and regulatory needs.

00:46:43.540 --> 00:46:47.430
So we recognize that
most of you here probably

00:46:47.430 --> 00:46:51.720
have instances and employees
all around the world.

00:46:51.720 --> 00:46:54.660
Laws are different in
different countries.

00:46:54.660 --> 00:46:56.520
And you have to meet
those local laws.

00:46:56.520 --> 00:46:58.060
So we work very, very hard.

00:46:58.060 --> 00:47:00.300
And we have a ton
of certifications

00:47:00.300 --> 00:47:03.750
to show that we are complying
with all laws, all local laws,

00:47:03.750 --> 00:47:05.530
wherever you may be operating.

00:47:05.530 --> 00:47:08.580
So that's the second
piece about compliance

00:47:08.580 --> 00:47:09.715
and regulatory needs.

00:47:09.715 --> 00:47:11.340
And then the third
piece that comes up,

00:47:11.340 --> 00:47:14.290
which is there's
always technology,

00:47:14.290 --> 00:47:18.000
but we've also perfected and we
continue to be extremely open--

00:47:18.000 --> 00:47:19.720
there's actually a link that--

00:47:19.720 --> 00:47:22.080
I know you can't see it,
but I'm happy to share that

00:47:22.080 --> 00:47:23.280
after the talk.

00:47:23.280 --> 00:47:25.710
But we are publicly
documenting everything

00:47:25.710 --> 00:47:28.300
that we do, not just
from a tech standpoint,

00:47:28.300 --> 00:47:30.780
but also from a
process standpoint.

00:47:30.780 --> 00:47:33.270
Like I mentioned
previously, our data centers

00:47:33.270 --> 00:47:36.510
are custom-built all the
way from the hardware

00:47:36.510 --> 00:47:39.150
to the network to the software.

00:47:39.150 --> 00:47:41.250
And they're hardened
systems, right?

00:47:41.250 --> 00:47:43.530
But more importantly,
what happens to employees?

00:47:43.530 --> 00:47:48.810
And what are the access policies
for a Google employee that's

00:47:48.810 --> 00:47:50.750
working in our data center?

00:47:50.750 --> 00:47:52.400
So it's publicly documented.

00:47:52.400 --> 00:47:53.550
We have background checks.

00:47:53.550 --> 00:47:55.110
We have independent audits.

00:47:55.110 --> 00:47:57.000
So please do go
through that as well.

00:47:57.000 --> 00:48:00.836
If you're interested, everything
is available on our website.

00:48:00.836 --> 00:48:02.460
So it's really
important that we think,

00:48:02.460 --> 00:48:04.860
not just from a tech standpoint,
but also from a process

00:48:04.860 --> 00:48:07.800
standpoint, we think this
is a pretty hardened system.

00:48:07.800 --> 00:48:10.931
And I really cannot go in and
just start looking for data.

00:48:10.931 --> 00:48:12.180
That is, everything is logged.

00:48:12.180 --> 00:48:13.596
I would actually
get permission, I

00:48:13.596 --> 00:48:15.950
would have justification,
all those kinds of things.

00:48:15.950 --> 00:48:18.240
And I actually can't
access data just

00:48:18.240 --> 00:48:19.950
because I'm a Google employee.

00:48:19.950 --> 00:48:21.340
So please do read up on that.

00:48:21.340 --> 00:48:24.480
Anytime somebody says
something to the effect

00:48:24.480 --> 00:48:28.375
that we are using your data for
other reasons, needs to realize

00:48:28.375 --> 00:48:29.250
that is not the case.

00:48:29.250 --> 00:48:33.000
And please do check up on
where we are at online.

00:48:33.000 --> 00:48:36.330
I'm happy to have a
conversation offline as well.

00:48:36.330 --> 00:48:37.560
So this is my last slide.

00:48:37.560 --> 00:48:40.790
And we think about
Google's IDaaS solution,

00:48:40.790 --> 00:48:43.440
at least we think that
we can bring our security

00:48:43.440 --> 00:48:45.270
to run your
business-critical cases.

00:48:45.270 --> 00:48:48.385
So now our security is at
work becoming your security.

00:48:48.385 --> 00:48:49.260
That's the key thing.

00:48:49.260 --> 00:48:51.900
Second, you saw this,
which is everything we do

00:48:51.900 --> 00:48:53.460
is open and standards-based.

00:48:53.460 --> 00:48:56.190
In fact, the enterprise
security keys

00:48:56.190 --> 00:49:00.270
that we talked about, we worked
together with the FIDO Alliance

00:49:00.270 --> 00:49:02.440
and we helped create
that standard.

00:49:02.440 --> 00:49:04.830
And that's the standard
that anybody is

00:49:04.830 --> 00:49:06.880
open to using and implementing.

00:49:06.880 --> 00:49:10.090
So we're not here to keep
our innovation to ourselves.

00:49:10.090 --> 00:49:12.330
But we want to contribute
back for the greater

00:49:12.330 --> 00:49:13.660
good of the community.

00:49:13.660 --> 00:49:15.670
The third is, you saw
Ameet's demo, which

00:49:15.670 --> 00:49:16.690
is 100% self-service.

00:49:16.690 --> 00:49:19.560
So those of you who
are in the audience

00:49:19.560 --> 00:49:22.620
that have been using our
Google Cloud Platform

00:49:22.620 --> 00:49:24.740
and you've had
to, unfortunately,

00:49:24.740 --> 00:49:27.150
live with some of the
pain of an offline

00:49:27.150 --> 00:49:29.460
sign up and offline
provisioning mainly

00:49:29.460 --> 00:49:32.060
taking days before you
can be up and running,

00:49:32.060 --> 00:49:34.710
we now have a 100% self-service.

00:49:34.710 --> 00:49:37.380
And it is not tied
to G Suite, right?

00:49:37.380 --> 00:49:40.080
You can use it for your other
G Suite users if you want.

00:49:40.080 --> 00:49:41.462
That was a problem as well.

00:49:41.462 --> 00:49:42.420
So now you can do that.

00:49:42.420 --> 00:49:43.880
You can use it for GCP.

00:49:43.880 --> 00:49:46.929
And this'll just extend,
because it is the same identity

00:49:46.929 --> 00:49:48.970
if you're thinking of
Chromebooks or other Google

00:49:48.970 --> 00:49:49.470
services.

00:49:49.470 --> 00:49:50.739
So that's number three.

00:49:50.739 --> 00:49:52.780
And the fourth is, your
trust is important to us.

00:49:52.780 --> 00:49:56.500
So we do a variety of things,
including meeting local laws,

00:49:56.500 --> 00:50:00.850
but also making sure that we are
very transparent about our data

00:50:00.850 --> 00:50:02.990
access policies
and what have you,

00:50:02.990 --> 00:50:05.980
so you understand how we
treat your data if you do move

00:50:05.980 --> 00:50:08.180
to Google Cloud Identity.

00:50:08.180 --> 00:50:10.330
